  "text_md": "(4) The privilege of turning out cattle on commons. North. A oblong square board fixed to a\n\nAs a lopwebbe filetli fome and gnattis. Taken and suffren gret files go. Occleve, MS. Soc. Jntiq. 134,\n\nOccleve, MS Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 267. LOQUINTUE Eloquent. Weber\n\nLOOT. thin staff or handle, used in boiling brine to remove the scum. Staff.\n\nLORD. (1) A title of given to monks and persons of superior rank. (4.-s) honour\n\nLOOTH. The same as 9 Loo,\n\ninscription on houses infected with the plague. Lord have mercy upon me, a disease thus mentioned in the Nomenclator, \"the IlUake passion, or a paine and wringing in the small guts, which the homelier sort of phisicians doe call, Lorde have mercy upon me.\" LORDEYN. See FeverLicrden. \" The lurgy-\n\nLOOVER. An opening at the of a dove-cote. North. See Lover (2). top\n\nLOOVEYD. Praised. Ritson.\n\nLOOVEYNG. Praise honour.\n\nThat was a feyre tokenynge\n\nOf pees and of looveyng.\n\nMS:\n\nLORDEYN_ See Fever-Lurden. The fever, idleness; Craven Glossary, p. 304. trow he was infecte certeyn\n\nLOOZE.\n\nFf. ii. 38, f. A pig-stye. West. L003. Laughed. \" At hym ful fast thei MS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 53. LOP. A North. {A.-S.)\n\n(1) flea. Ys joy y-now so ye your lyggys streyne, Ye lade longe-sydyde as a loppe. MS. Fairfax\n\nMS. Rawl. C . 86, xv. Cent.\n\nLORD-FEST. Excessively lordly. (A.-S.) LORDINGS. Sirs ; masters. (A.~S.) It is often used bv later writers in contempt. LORD-OF-MISRULE. The person who pre-\n\nMS. Fairfar 16.\n\nTo lounge about. Kent. To loosely to down, or Var. dial. lollop hang droop. hang\n\nsided over the Christmas revels, by no means an unimportant personage in the olden times. He began his rule on All-hallow eve and continued it till Candlemas day. See a hst of expences, dated in 1552, in Kempe's Loseley Manuscripts, pp. 44-54. For further information on the subject, see Brand, i. 272 ; Arch, xviii, 313-335 ; Hawkins' Engl. Dram. iii. 156; Strutt, ii. 200 ; Lilly's Sixe Court Comedies, 12mo. 1632, sig. F.\n\nThe faggot wood of a tree.\n\nAlso, to It seems to be\n\nLeapt.\n\nsubst. in the second example.\n\nAs sone as the 'chylde had spoke,\n\nThe fende ynto hym was lope.\n\nUS. Harl. 1701, f. 40.\n\nTyme goth fast, it is full And in abydyng men seyn ther lyghte hope. MS. Rawl. Poet. 118.\n\nMS. Rawl Poet. 118.\n\nLOP-EARED. Having long pendulous ears Uke abound. Var. dial. LOPEN. Leapt. See the Sevyn Sages, 739.\n\nLORDS-AND-LADIES. See Bulls-and-Cows. LORDSHIP. Supreme power. (J.-S.) LORD-SIZE. The judge at the assizes.\n\nLOPEN. Leapt. Sec the Sevyn Sages, 739. Whan thy mouthe with shryfte ys opun Deth and synne are bothe oute lopun.\n\nLORD'S-ROOM.\n\nThe stage-box in a theatre was formerly so called. Jonson. LORDSWYK. A traitor. Eifson.\n\nMS. Harl. 1701, f. 79.\n\nLORDSWYK A traitor. Ritson.\n\nThe portar set the yatys opon,\n\nLORE. (1) Knowledge ; doctrine advice. ( A.-S) (2) Lost. Still in use in Somerset.\n\nAnd with that Befyse ys owt lopon.\n\nCantab Ff. ii. 38, f. 108.\n\nAnoon was al that feire gederynge Lopen undir oure lordes wynge.\n\nThe kyng seld, Take me thy tayle,\n\nFor my hors wolde not the fayle,\n\nA peny that thou lore.\n\nSythen he ys lopen on hys stede,\n\nFf. v. 48, f. 61. LORE FAD YR. A teacher. Loremastir, Dial. Great. Moral, p. 243. Of al men they do most evy].\n\nHe with hym Harrawde dud lede.\n\nMS: Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 154. LOPE-STAFF. leaping-staff: lope-staffe wherewith men leape ditches, Cotgrave:\n\nOf al men they do most evyl,\n\nHere lorefadyr ys thedevyl. MS. Harl. 1701, f 24, LOREINE. A rcin. Sce Lannfal , 888\n\nLOPIRD.   Coagulated. Still iu use.  See Forby, Brockett, Grose, Kennett, &c.\n\nHys loreine lemyd alle with pride, Stede and armure alle was blake\n\nThare he fande none other fode,\n\nBot wlatesome glcte and lopird blcde\n\n2lf5:. 22.52, f. 104.\n\nLOREL. A bad worthless fellow. {A.-N.) Lorels den, Holinshed, Chron. Ireland, p. 93. Cocke Lorel was formerly a generic title for a very great rascal. \" Lasy lorrels,\" Harman, 1567. LOREMAR. A bit-maker. Palsgrave. \"Lori-\n\nLincoln A.l.\n\nThare dwelled a\n\nman\n\nAnd In myrke donjowne, in fowle stede of corrupcyowne,\n\nWhare he had no fode,\n\nBot wlatsome glette and lopyrd blode.\n\nLOREMAR bit-maker. Palsgrave. Lori . mers or bit-makers; Harrison; p. 97. LORENGE. Iron.\n\np. 25. LOP-LOACH. The leech used by surgeons for drawing blood. North. LOPLOLLY. A lazy fellow. West.\n\nA-N:) LORER. The laurel-tree. Chaucer.\n\nLOPLOLLY. A lazy fellow . West.\n\nLOPPING. Lame. Dorset.\n\nThís Daphne into a lorer tre\n\nLOP-SIDED. Var. dial\n\nWas turnid, whiche is ever grene.\n\nLOP-START. The stoat. East . It is mentioned in Harrison's England, P. 230.\n\nAnd plaunted trees that wcre to prelse,\n\nLOPUSTER A lobster:\n\nLOPWEBBE. 5 web. (4-s) II spider'\n\n",
